o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o
| 2 Expectations                                                              |
o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o

  The Cal3D library is licensed under the LGPL (GNU Lesser General Public
  License) which, among other things, allows you to link proprietary
  applications with it. The Cal3D team strongly believes in the Free Software
  movement and provides this library without any additional restrictions.
  However, to make this a "fair deal" and to support the future development of
  the library, we ask you to follow these points when using the Cal3D library
  in your projects:

    - Seriously consider releasing you project under a free license yourself.
    - Read and understand the LGPL and make sure that your usage of Cal3D
      complies with it.
    - Include a prominent mention about the usage of the Cal3D library in
      your project.
    - Send a short message to us when you release your work.
    - Provide feedback to the Cal3D project to help us enhance it.
    - If you make millions with your project, donate some bucks to the Cal3D
      project to fund the future development =)

  Furthermore, here are a few other possibilities to help the Cal3D project:

    - The Cal3D is always in need of models, animations, textures and demos.
      So, if you have something to spare, send it along!
    - Sponsoring and donations can definitively help the project, just
      contact us.
    - Have you heard of a new animation technology? Is there a freshly
      written, earth-shaking paper about inverse-kinematics on your desk?
      Fell free to send it to the Cal3D project, so we can put it into the
      library!

  The Cal3D team thanks you for your support and wishes you success with all
  your projects!


o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o
| 3 What is 'cal3d'?                                                          |
o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o

  Cal3D is a skeletal based character animation library. It is platform-
  independent and not bound to a specific graphic API. Originally designed to
  be used in a 3d client for the Worldforge project ( www.worldforge.org )
  it evolved into a stand-alone product that can be used in many different
  projects.


o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o
| 4 Features                                                                  |
o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o

  - skeletal based animation setup
  - seamless blending of multiple animation tracks
  - attachment and detachment of one or more skinned meshes at runtime
  - transformation of vertices and normals according to the weighted influence
    of one or more bones
  - flexible material handling
  - support for multiple texture mapping channels
  - progressive meshes for LOD (level-of-detail)
  - binary and text file formats
  - experimental spring-system module for cloth and hair animation
  - unified exporter plugin framework (3D Studio Max, MilkShape 3D and others
    following)
  - highly portable and modular design (only depends on C++ and STL)
  - simple and clean API (both in C++ and "pure" C)
  - did I mention that it is free? (see the LGPL license)


o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o
| 5 Requirements                                                              |
o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o

  There are no special requirements to compile/use cal3d. A C++ compiler with
  (decent) STL support should be enough.


o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o
| 6 Website                                                                   |
o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o

  The official website of cal3d can be found at: http://cal3d.sourceforge.net


o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o
| 7 Author                                                                    |
o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o

  This project was originally started by Bruno 'Beosil' Heidelberger. You can
  find a list of all people contributing to cal3d in the AUTHORS file.


o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o
| 8 Model-space animation compression                                         |
o-----------------------------------------------------------------------------o

  The legacy CalCoreTrack::compress() judges a keyframe against a local
  translation distance and a local rotation angle.  That can discard a
  sub-degree swing on a bone near the root while the end of a long bone chain
  ends up displaced by a whole model unit.

  CalCoreAnimation::compressModelSpace() offers an optional entry point that
  instead controls whole-character deformation.  It leaves the original
  CalCoreTrack::compress(), optimize() and the file formats untouched.

  Error units
  -----------
  The error budget (maxPositionError) and the reported maxError are Euclidean
  distances in model-space position units - the same units as the skeleton's
  bone translations and the bind-pose probe points (typically meters or
  whatever unit the asset was authored in).  They are not angles and are not
  split per bone: one budget applies to the whole character.  A limit of 0 is
  legal; exactly redundant keyframes (identical poses, or a quaternion and its
  negation describing the same orientation) can still be removed.

  Discrete sampling range
  ------------------------
  The guarantee is deliberately defined only over a fixed discrete set of
  times, never over every continuous instant.  The set is computed once, up
  front, and never shrinks as keyframes are deleted:

    0, the animation duration, every keyframe time of every original track,
    the midpoint of each pair of consecutive times in that sorted, de-duplicated
    list, and any extra sample times supplied by the caller (also required to
    lie in [0, duration]).

  At every such time each probe point is skinned with linear-blend skinning,
  using the evaluated CalSkeleton bone transforms (model transform times the
  bone's inverse bind pose transform), exactly as CalPhysique skins a vertex.
  The error is the largest distance between a probe skinned from the original
  animation and the same probe skinned from a candidate animation.  A probe
  holds a bind-pose point plus boneId/weight pairs; weights must be
  non-negative and sum to 1.  Bones without a track keep their bind pose; an
  empty track still contributes an identity local transform, and the two are
  not treated as equivalent.

  Only interior keyframes of existing tracks can be deleted.  Each non-empty
  track keeps its first and last key, its surviving key times and the original
  key values; no keys are inserted and no interpolator is changed.  Each round
  deletes the single interior key whose removal yields the smallest global
  error while the global error stays within budget (ties broken by bone id,
  then key time), and re-validates the complete candidate afterwards, so
  deletions that are legal separately but illegal together are rejected.

  Example
  -------

    #include <cal3d/coreanimation.h>
    #include <cal3d/modelspacecompression.h>

    // skeleton is an already-fixuped CalCoreSkeleton; clip is the animation.
    cal3d::ModelSpaceProbe handProbe;
    handProbe.point = CalVector(0.1f, -0.2f, 0.05f); // bind-pose position
    handProbe.boneIds.push_back(wristBoneId);       // single-bone probe
    handProbe.weights.push_back(1.0f);

    cal3d::ModelSpaceProbe shoulderProbe;
    shoulderProbe.point = CalVector(0, 0, 0);       // multi-bone probe
    shoulderProbe.boneIds.push_back(upperArmBoneId);
    shoulderProbe.boneIds.push_back(clavicleBoneId);
    shoulderProbe.weights.push_back(0.7f);
    shoulderProbe.weights.push_back(0.3f);

    std::vector<cal3d::ModelSpaceProbe> probes;
    probes.push_back(handProbe);
    probes.push_back(shoulderProbe);

    std::vector<float> extraTimes;
    extraTimes.push_back(0.33f); // force this instant into the sample set

    // Keep every skinned probe within 0.5 model-space position units.
    cal3d::ModelSpaceCompressionResult result =
        clip->compressModelSpace(skeleton, probes, extraTimes, 0.5);

    // result.animation is an independent CalCoreAnimation; the inputs are
    // unmodified.  result.report describes what was removed and measured:
    //   removedKeyframes      - number of interior keys deleted
    //   maxError              - largest skinned-point distance observed
    //   maxErrorTime          - sample time of that worst distance
    //   maxErrorProbeIndex    - probe index of that worst distance

  Invalid input (duplicate track bone ids, non-strictly-increasing or
  out-of-range key times, unknown bones, non-finite numbers, weights that are
  negative, non-finite or do not sum to 1, or a negative error limit) throws
  std::runtime_error before any result is produced, leaving all inputs
  unchanged.


